--TEST--
A `foreach` binding and a `catch` binding inside a closure declare locals of that closure, so one spelled the same as a binding of the enclosing body shadows it instead of capturing it, pinning `rule:types/implicit-capture` and `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/closures
// A capture is recorded for a name the closure *reads* from the body around
// it. Declaring a name is not reading one: the binding a `foreach` or a
// `catch` introduces belongs to the closure, whatever the enclosing body
// happens to have called its own.
class Log {
    public static int $outer = 0;
    public static string $caught = "";
}

array<int> $first = [1, 2];
foreach ($first as int $v) {
    Log::$outer = Log::$outer + $v;
}

var $inner = fn (): int => {
    array<int> $second = [3, 4];
    int $total = 0;
    foreach ($second as int $v) {
        $total = $total + $v;
    }
    return $total;
};
echo Log::$outer, " ", $inner(), "\n";

try {
    throw new RuntimeError("from the body");
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    Log::$caught = $e->message;
}

var $said = fn (): string => {
    string $message = "nothing thrown";
    try {
        throw new LogicError("from the closure");
    } catch (LogicError $e) {
        $message = $e->message;
    }
    return $message;
};
echo Log::$caught, " / ", $said(), "\n";

// The enclosing binding is untouched by either closure, and still reads as
// what the body last put in it.
foreach ($first as int $v) {
    Log::$outer = Log::$outer + $v;
}
echo Log::$outer, "\n";
--EXPECT--
3 7
from the body / from the closure
6
